/// ⚠️ PRISMA-SCHEMA DRIFT WARNING ⚠️
/// TWO Postgres objects on this model are raw SQL in the migration:
///
///   1. `embedding halfvec(1536)` — the pgvector column type. Probe B2 asserts
///      the column is `halfvec` and not merely present: `columnExists` cannot
///      tell the two apart, and a column of this name holding `vector` doubles
///      the largest object in the database while every query keeps working.
///   2. `GENERATED ALWAYS AS (...) STORED` on `searchVector`. Probe B6.
///
/// `halfvec`, not `vector`, since 2026-08-25 (scale.md S1). Half the bytes per
/// dimension for a recall difference that does not show up on normalised
/// embeddings, and at the target scale the table is the single largest object
/// in the database: 800 GB of `vector(1536)` is 400 GB of `halfvec(1536)` at
/// 100k users. The narrowing to 1024 dimensions (S2) is a further third and is
/// blocked on Resparkable owning its own `AiProviderModel` row, because the
/// platform knowledge base shares the active model and its own column is
/// `vector(1536) NOT NULL`.
///
/// TWO INDEXES WERE DELIBERATELY REMOVED and probes B3 and B7 now assert they
/// stay gone (scale.md S3). Do not "restore" them:
///
///   - HNSW `idx_framework_resparkable_embedding_hnsw` — `hybridSearchRows`
///     cannot use it. The distance pre-filter and the blended `ORDER BY
///     final_score` both defeat it, which the query's own doc comment has said
///     since it was written. pgvector's HNSW stores a full copy of every vector,
///     so it cost roughly the size of the table plus a graph traversal on every
///     insert, in exchange for nothing.
///   - GIN `idx_framework_resparkable_embedding_search_vector` — same story.
///     The query computes `ts_rank_cd` over the already-`userId`-filtered set
///     and carries no `@@` predicate, so the index is never consulted.
///
/// Both come back with the inner-CTE rewrite (S4) and not before. That rewrite
/// changes recall semantics, so it is a decision, not a tidy-up.
///
/// ONE vector table for all entity types (D2) — one column, one re-embed path,
/// one search query. That cuts the pgvector drift surface roughly five-fold
/// versus a vector column per entity table.
///
/// Archiving DELETES the rows here rather than filtering them at query time
/// (§11, §17 risk 5b).
model ResparkableEmbedding {
  id      String @id @default(cuid())
  spaceId String

  /// Who wrote this row (§23.5). Null in every space that existed before groups
  /// did, and redundant with the owner in a personal space; in a group space it
  /// is what makes attribution and the activity feed possible at all.
  ///
  /// ⚠️ PRISMA-SCHEMA DRIFT WARNING ⚠️
  /// The FK to `"user"("id")` ON DELETE **SetNull** is hand-written (Prisma
  /// cannot model it: no relation field may be added to `User`). Probe B11
  /// covers this column on every satellite at once.
  ///
  /// SetNull rather than Cascade is the point: erasing a member removes their
  /// authorship and leaves the group's content, because that content is the
  /// group's. Note the asymmetry with `ResparkableComment.authorUserId`, which
  /// genuinely cascades — authored rows survive an erasure and comment bodies
  /// do not.
  createdByUserId String?

  /// thought | project | goal | area | entity | document. NOT task (§1).
  entityType String @db.VarChar(16)
  entityId   String
  chunkIndex Int    @default(0)

  content String @db.Text

  /// Denormalised from the source row, at write time (Release 1.5, phase 9e).
  ///
  /// **This is the only read path that could not otherwise express the
  /// private/shareable distinction.** Every other read goes through a repo
  /// function that can filter the source table; the vector pass ranks chunks
  /// and never touches it, so before this column a `sensitive` thought was a
  /// candidate for `resparkable-triage` (unattended, 03:00) and for
  /// `resparkable-strategist`, which writes `ResparkableReview` bodies — and
  /// reviews are on §13's shareable list. That is a private-to-shareable path,
  /// and it existed before sharing did.
  ///
  /// Only `thought` rows can be anything but `'private'` today; every other
  /// embedded type takes the default. Kept in sync by `updateThought`, which
  /// writes here in the same transaction rather than waiting for a reindex — a
  /// privacy control that takes effect at the next nightly pass is not a
  /// privacy control. **Never part of `contentHash`**: sensitivity is not
  /// semantic content, and folding it in would re-embed the corpus every time
  /// someone reclassified a note.
  sensitivity String @default("private") @db.VarChar(16)

  embedding    Unsupported("halfvec(1536)")?
  searchVector Unsupported("tsvector")?

  /// Hash of SEMANTIC CONTENT ONLY — never rendered markdown, or every
  /// frontmatter tick re-embeds the corpus (§17 risk 3).
  contentHash String

  /// When the connection sweep last examined this entity as a source.
  ///
  /// The sweep is capped per type per run, and this is what makes the cap rotate
  /// instead of re-examining the same head of the list for ever. Ordering by
  /// `embeddedAt desc` (the obvious choice) would leave everything past the cap
  /// permanently unreachable, because `embeddedAt` only moves on a re-embed.
  /// Null = never swept, which sorts first.
  sweptAt DateTime?

  embeddingModel     String?
  embeddingProvider  String?
  /// Probed by `assertActiveModelMatchesStoredVectors()` — without it a model
  /// swap becomes a cryptic SQL cast crash (§17 risk 5).
  embeddingDimension Int?
  embeddedAt         DateTime?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  /// D1: the cascade path. Erasing a user removes the space row, which
  /// removes every row here. Verified by scripts/smoke/resparkable-isolation.ts.
  space ResparkableSpace @relation(fields: [spaceId], references: [spaceId], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([spaceId, entityType, entityId, chunkIndex])
  @@index([spaceId, entityType])
  @@index([spaceId, contentHash])
  @@map("framework_resparkable_embedding")
}

